<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicias</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 470–<a href="/years/413-bce">413 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Nikias</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicias was an Athenian politician and general prominent in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>, a wealthy aristocrat with interests in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laurion">Laurion</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> says he owned a thousand men. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> in 429 BCE he led the aristocrats as the principal rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleon">Cleon</a>, opposing the aggressive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> of Cleon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcibiades">Alcibiades</a>, and served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> with a reputation for over-caution. He was largely responsible for the Peace of Nicias with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> in 421. Appointed in 415 BCE to command the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-expedition">Sicilian Expedition</a>, he delayed the withdrawal after a lunar eclipse, surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gylippus">Gylippus</a> at the Assinaros river and was executed by the Syracusans in 413.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicias.jpg" alt="Nicias" width="330" height="471" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Jennings Bryan, Francis Whiting Halsey · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicias,_p_105_(World's_Famous_Orations_Vol_1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A great fortune lay in land, houses, silver and slaves: the general <b>Nicias</b> hired out a thousand slaves to the silver mines of Laurion.</blockquote><a href="/societies/athens">Social Hierarchy of Classical Athens</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Syracuse, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.98,23.73&amp;year=-433" aria-label="Athens on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5141 798 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nicias"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5591 1068h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.98,23.73&amp;year=-433">What was here in 433 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Men make the city and not walls or ships without men in them.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to the army in retreat from Syracuse (Thucydides, book 7) &#183; tr. Richard Crawley, 1874 &#183; Thucydides' account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7142/pg7142-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
